::: {.doc-subject}

Build the moral graph of a directed graph: marry the parents, drop the directions.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L68413-L68520){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.moral_graph()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Moral graph](../assets/graphs/moral-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: the sprinkler network, a directed graph in which cloudy points to rain and sprinkler, and both rain and sprinkler point to wet grass. Right: moral_graph() marries the two parents of wet by joining rain and sprinkler with a new edge (accent color) and drops every direction, giving an undirected graph with five edges." width="100%"}

The moral graph joins every pair of nodes that share a child ("marrying" the parents) and
then forgets edge directions. It is the first step of exact inference in Bayesian
networks (before triangulation into a junction tree, see `complete_to_chordal_graph()`),
because each node and its parents must end up in a common clique, and its separation
properties encode the conditional independences of the network.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A new undirected graph with the same nodes (IDs, labels and attributes kept), one edge
    per adjacent pair of the original (parallel and opposite edges merged, weights kept
    from the first edge) and one unweighted edge per pair of co-parents that were not
    already adjacent.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is undirected.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Every pair of in-neighbors of every node is joined, $O(\sum_v \deg^-(v)^2)$. Matches
NetworkX's `moral_graph` (edges compared as unordered pairs).

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~v_structures`) 

:   The parents that moralizing joins.

[](`~junction_tree`) 

:   A tree of cliques built from the moral graph.

[](`~is_d_separator`) 

:   Check if a set of nodes d-separates two others.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Moralizing a collider marries the two parents and drops the directions:

::: {#85cf0e0f .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "C"), ("B", "C")], directed=True)
graph.moral_graph().get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['A--C', 'B--C', 'A--B']
```
:::
:::


### Marrying the causes

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. Its moral graph has 16 edges: the 10 arrows without their
directions and 6 between parents with a common child, one for each v-structure. The
married pairs come after the original edges:

::: {#5bb6ab79 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal = ec.causal()
moral = causal.moral_graph()

moral.count_edges(), moral.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)[10:]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(16,
 ['Exercise--Diet',
  'Age--Weight',
  'Age--Genetics',
  'Weight--Medication',
  'Weight--Genetics',
  'Medication--Genetics'])
```
:::
:::


::: {#b26dd3d3 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
married = moral.get_edge_ids()[causal.count_edges():]
moral.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight_edges=married, label_by="label", height=360
)
```

